WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.270
 I guarantee if you invite your
friends over for fish and grits

00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:05.280
and you have some
crab in your gravy,

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:07.560
they'll be talking about
that for a little while.

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:09.970
My mother cooked the
best collard greens.

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:12.690
My grandmother cooked
better collard greens.

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:15.210
They were the best cooks I knew.

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:17.460
Because of them,
I ended up really

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:21.110
focusing on the food
in the Southern region.

00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:23.950
PRESENTER: And the Beard
Award goes to Mashama Bailey.

00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:25.583
[CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]

00:00:25.583 --> 00:00:27.500
MASHAMA BAILEY: When I
think of Southern food,

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:30.290
I think of Georgia, the
Appalachian Mountains,

00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:31.700
and the Gulf.

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:34.430
One of the most
common misconceptions

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:36.230
is that it's unhealthy.

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:41.390
But I'm here because I want
to demystify Southern food.

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:44.690
In this MasterClass, I'm going
to show you the rich layers

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:46.430
of cooking Southern food.

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:49.340
And we're going to focus
on different techniques,

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:52.500
like braising,
smoking, and pickling.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:55.290
We're going to smoke
the collard greens.

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:57.860
With this technique, you're
introducing the element

00:00:57.860 --> 00:00:59.780
of smoke without cooking them.

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:03.590
It smells like summertime
in Waynesboro, Georgia.

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:06.140
You really can't have
gumbo without okra.

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:09.230
For folks who don't like
okra because it's slimy,

00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:12.500
try charring it first
and then adding it.

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:14.630
The secret to the
gumbo is the roux.

00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:18.560
It not only adds texture,
it also adds flavor.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:23.030
It just feels like a big,
nutritious bowl of goodness.

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:27.950
Converting an old Greyhound
bus station that was segregated

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:32.720
and cooking at the helm
of it is really special.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:35.090
I often think about
my grandmothers,

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:39.380
who could not even roam through
a building like that freely.

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:42.170
So there's an obligation.

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:44.930
Being Black in
America, we weren't

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:46.640
able to document our recipes.

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:51.410
So we relied on oral histories
to keep our traditions alive.

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:54.620
And I'm really
embracing my own journey

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:57.830
and applying that to
the classical skills

00:01:57.830 --> 00:01:59.150
that I've learned.

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:01.910
Thing that I'm learning now
about cooking Southern food

00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:03.600
is that the sky's the limit.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:07.310
So don't be afraid to explore
your own personal cooking

00:02:07.310 --> 00:02:08.630
style.

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:13.300
I'm Mashama Bailey, and
this is my MasterClass.

